v0.19.0b
------
Transfers can now be edited instead of only delete-and-recreate — clicking ✎ Edit on either leg opens
the edit form with a warning banner ("This is a transfer — date, amount, and notes changes apply to
BOTH sides of it") and only those three fields exposed; saving keeps both legs in sync
(services/database/transactions.js's new updateTransferPair, controllers/transactionsController.js's
update()). Payee/category/splits/tags and which two accounts are involved still aren't editable
in place — Convert to transfer or delete-and-recreate cover changing those. Requires write access to
both accounts, same as creating a transfer does, so a readwrite share on just one side can't be used
to edit the other.

You can now create a schedule directly from an existing register entry — a new 📅 button next to
Edit/Delete on each row opens a quick-add modal right there, pre-filled with that entry's amount,
payee/category (or transfer account), and notes (public/js/register.js, accounts/show.ejs). The
next-date field is left blank on purpose rather than reusing the entry's own (already-past) date, so
an auto-enter schedule can't immediately re-post itself the moment you save it. Before saving, it
checks this account's existing schedules for one that already looks like the same recurring thing
(same transfer destination, or same payee and money direction) and warns you first, rather than
silently creating a duplicate — a second click on Create goes ahead anyway. Not offered for split
transactions — this dialog has no split UI to receive them; the full Schedules page still covers
autopay, email reminders, and weekday/day-of-month recurrence this quick version skips.

Rules can now be edited, not just created/deleted/toggled — the Rules page's "Add rule" form doubles
as an edit form (an Edit button per row, mirroring how Schedules already does it), since the server
side already supported updating a rule's name/priority/conditions/actions and only the UI was
missing it (public/js/rules.js, views/rules/index.ejs).

The same "create a schedule from this" quick-add modal above is now also reachable from a
transaction's own edit form ("extended" view, opened via the register's ✎ Edit button) — a new
"📅 Create schedule from this" button next to Convert to transfer, for whenever you're already in
there editing rather than starting from the row itself. Its new sibling, "📋 Create rule from this",
opens a matching quick-add-rule modal: one condition and one action (prefilled from the transaction's
payee/notes and category), with the same before-you-save check against existing rules with an
identical condition, warning rather than silently creating a duplicate. Both link out to the full
Rules/Schedules pages for anything more elaborate than a single condition/action.

Added a per-account "Show the Forecast chart expanded by default" setting (Account settings) — the
register's Forecast chart previously always opened expanded regardless of preference; a manual
Hide/Show click during a session still overrides this until you reload (models/account.js,
public/js/register.js, public/js/accounts.js).

Demo mode (DEMO_MODE=true) now seeds rules and schedules along with everything else a fresh demo
account already got — previously both pages sat empty. Each new demo account gets 3 rules that
actually fire against its own seeded payees (Amazon → Shopping, Coffee → Dining Out, Gas & Go →
Transportation) and 5 schedules covering every shape the app supports: a plain auto-entered
paycheck, an autopay bill, a reminder-only bill that never posts itself, an autopay subscription
with email notify, and a recurring transfer to savings (services/demo/seedDemoUserData.js).

Starting the demo now actually shows you your generated login. Previously /demo/start logged you
straight in with no way to ever see the randomly generated email/password it created — there was no
way back in if your session ever expired. A one-shot banner under the nav bar now shows both right
after signup (controllers/demoController.js, server.js, views/components/nav.ejs), same pattern
loadoutwaypoints already used, then clears itself so it doesn't linger on every later page load.

Fixed the licensing gate for a brand-new install: LICENSE_SERVER_URL now defaults to the real
allthewaypoints licensing service (https://licensing.allthewaypoints.com) instead of requiring it be
set by hand, and dropped the now-vestigial LICENSE_API_SECRET requirement — that service's /api/trial
and /api/validate are public now (email-gated and rate-limited on its side instead), matching
loadoutwaypoints (config/licensing.js, services/licensing/client.js). Existing installs that had
LICENSE_SERVER_URL/LICENSE_API_SECRET set by hand are unaffected; new installs no longer need either.

prod.yml now also builds and publishes a Docker image on every push to master — tagged with both
:latest and the exact package.json version, pushed to both the internal Gitea registry and GHCR
(.gitea/workflows/prod.yml). This is what makes dist-example/docker-compose.yml and
docker-compose.pull.yml (which reference ghcr.io/j5guy/balancedwaypoints:latest) actually work as
written; previously nothing published that image. Needs new/renamed repo secrets — see the CI/CD
wiki page.

v0.18.0b
------
Deployment rebuilt from scratch around a single, self-contained docker-compose.yml — no more shared
Traefik reverse proxy, no waypoints-proxy network, no .env file, no guided setup wizard. Replaces
install.sh/update.sh/uninstall.sh/scripts/setup-wizard.js/scripts/migrate-to-traefik.js and the
docker-compose.mongo.yml/docker-compose.tls-acme.yml overlay files entirely, matching how
loadoutwaypoints already deploys: MongoDB bundled in the same compose file, every setting as a plain
inline environment variable with a working default, `docker compose up -d` and nothing else. See
dist-example/docker-compose.yml for the file a new install actually downloads, and the wiki's
Installation Guide for the full rundown. Existing installs need to redeploy onto the new compose
file by hand (the old toolchain is gone) — the underlying MongoDB data volume carries over
regardless of compose shape.

The app now serves plain HTTP by default instead of assuming Traefik terminates HTTPS in front of
it. TLS is now optional and entirely self-contained: Admin > Settings has a new TLS section where
uploading a certificate and private key switches this process to terminating HTTPS itself, on the
same port, without a container restart (it swaps its own in-process listener) — or keep running
plain HTTP behind your own reverse proxy instead, same as before. Session and CSRF cookies adapt
automatically (`secure: 'auto'`/a plain, non-`__Host-`-prefixed CSRF cookie) so login and forms keep
working correctly under plain HTTP, self-terminated HTTPS, or a reverse proxy, instead of assuming
HTTPS is always present. The session secret is now baked into the image at build time and persisted
in a new secrets-data volume — nothing to hand-generate and put in a .env file anymore. The web
port is now configurable via a PORT env var instead of a fixed 5570, and MONGO_URI is now supported
as a direct override of the mongoHost/mongoPort/mongoUser/mongoPass block (for mongodb+srv://
/Atlas-style hosts).

Dropped OIDC/SSO support entirely, for the same deployment-simplicity reasons — local accounts and
LDAP remain, unaffected. Breaking change: any account that was using OIDC login has no other way to
sign back in (no password, no LDAP username) — recreate that person's login with
`node scripts/createUser.js <email> --password <password>` after updating, or have them sign up
fresh, before dropping their old account from Admin > Users.

v0.17.0b
------
Added an optional demo mode (DEMO_MODE=true, off by default — no effect on a normal self-hosted or cloud
install) for running a public try-it-yourself instance. A /demo page lets an anonymous visitor spin up an
ordinary, non-admin account with a randomly generated login (controllers/demoController.js) and is logged
straight in — no signup, no email required. Unlike a shared demo pool, each new demo account gets its own
freshly generated private starter dataset — a few accounts, categories, payees, budget assignments, and a
couple months of transaction history (services/demo/seedDemoUserData.js) — so nothing is ever shared
between demo users; editing your own copy never affects anyone else's. Rate-limited per IP
(middleware/demoRateLimit.js) to keep the database from filling up with junk between resets.

The whole database is wiped every night at 03:00 (services/demo/scheduler.js/reset.js) — a full
dropDatabase() rather than an actual container recreation, so there's no downtime — and once more at
every process boot. The license doc is snapshotted and restored across the wipe so a demo box doesn't come
back up gated behind /license with nothing to validate. Since this app had no existing admin-bootstrap
script (unlike a from-scratch signup, which always makes the first user an admin), added
services/bootstrap/createInitialAdmin.js: it recreates a persistent operator login from a new
ADMIN_PASSWORD env var (alongside the existing ADMIN_EMAIL) right after each reset, so whoever runs the
demo deployment can always get back in.

Also shortened the header logo text from "Balanced Waypoints" to just "Balanced" — the browser tab title
is unchanged.

